  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/b/b4/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_119.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A crowd below feels threatened by a seemingly out-of-control Human Torch, and as they discuss why, the readers propose that it is because:\n[HANG] 1) his girlfriend Doris Evans just jilted him for another guy\n[HANG] 2) he was prevented from joining the high school football team\n[HANG] 3) the other members of the FF went on vacation and left him behind so he wouldn't miss any school\n[HANG] 4) he keeps reading about Spider-Man's exploits in the newspaper\n[HANG] 5) and finally, a new character named the \"Rabble Rouser\" has been lambasting him all over town\n\nIndeed, the so-called \"Rabble Rouser\" seems to have a natural talent for arousing his listeners, even to \"enflame them,\" questioning the influence the Human Torch has over their youth, and finally swaying them to turn against their local hero.&nbsp; Only then do we learn that he does this with the aid of his \"will-sapping wand\" (later referred to as a \"mesmerizing wand\"), which helps <i>keep</i> them in this state.\n\nThe build-up against Johnny is so massive that even Spider-Man shows up to try and help, reasoning that he knows a thing or two about the subject -- Jameson does it to him all the time!&nbsp; But the hot-headed teenager tells him to buzz off and mind his own business (though he feels bad about the brush off moments afterward).\n\nTo make matters worse, Johnny is informed by a police officer that the Rabble Rouser has even convinced the city council to \"pass an ordinance against your flaming on without written permission from the mayor!\"&nbsp; To Johnny, this is the last straw -- he won't even be able to fight alongside the FF without a document!&nbsp; To the amazement of the officer, Johnny flames on and flies out of town, knowing full well the uniformed man won't be able to follow.&nbsp; He streaks toward New Jersey and out of town, officially becoming a \"fugitive,\" and as he departs, the Rabble Rouser seizes on the opportunity to drive home his point about how the Torch apparently believes he is above the law!\n\nOnly after the Rabble Rouser subsequently returns to his \"Greenwich Village lab\" does the reader learn that he is actually \"an undercover Red agent\" who's out to fool the people of America and hopefully become like another Castro!&nbsp; He plans to snatch a visiting foreign dignitary -- Prince Nagamo -- using a version of the underground \"red prototype sub-surface vehicle\" to capture him, trying to convince the Americans to stop giving aid to foreign nations.\n\nThe planned foreign dignitary arrives as planned, but his drive through town is interrupted by the arrival of the sub-surface vehicle, which somehow then hovers into the air and abducts the prince!&nbsp; The bullets of the police only bounce off its surface.&nbsp; They are completely unable to stop it.\n\nJohnny has returned to NYC and is actually on the scene, forced to stand idly by because of the new \"no flame\" city ordinance.&nbsp; Unable to fly into action, he can only watch as the police try in vain to stop the metallic craft from burrowing its way back down into the \"abandoned subway tunnels\" under the streets.\n\nOnly when the mayor finally gives him permission to flame on does the Human Torch finally ignite and fly down into the tunnel in pursuit.&nbsp; It isn't long before he tracks down the craft.&nbsp; The Rabble Rouser tries to use his wand to sap Johnny's will while he is temporarily unable to maintain his flame, but a timely intervention from the forgotten Prince Nagamo provides the time Johnny needs to subdue him <i>with the villain's own wand</i>, forcing him to \"love America!\"\n\nThe Rabble Rouser is captured, the visiting dignitary is returned, the mesmerizing wand is melted, and the city ordinance is amended to allow Johnny to fight crime.&nbsp; Even Doris Evans admits to Johnny how she was only trying to make him jealous.&nbsp; The [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved].",
  "grade_one_liner": "The Human Torch faces a master manipulator capable of swaying an entire city.\nUnfortunately, his mesmerizing wand couldn't hypnotize this plot into working.",
  "subtitle": "Will-Sapping in More Ways Than One",
  "review_text": "There is little of redeeming quality here, even if you consider the novel way the story opened, which is the crowd on the city streets below ticking off reasons they believe the Human Torch has been sent into a frenzy.\n\nWhether any of the reasons above the fifth one I listed in the plot summary are actually <b><i>true</i></b> (as opposed to mere speculation by the crowd) -- [SPAN: severity=info; text=is not certain].&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I mean, how on Earth can the crowd know that Johnny's girlfriend jilted him?&nbsp; Was that <i>really </i>covered in the \"gossip column?\"&nbsp; That's hard to believe!)]\n\nNevertheless, it sets the stage early on to introduce the new \"Rabble Rouser\" character, [SPAN: severity=success; text=which at the <i>very least</i> catapults the story into motion.]\n\nWith only 13 pages available, the story had to elevate the stakes such that Johnny was turned into a fugitive by the bottom of page 3!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=It was an <i>astonishing</i> achievement in plot acceleration, and you almost have to admire the sheer <i>audacity</i> of it!]\n\n[ASSET: left | RabbleRousersMasterPlan | width=12%] [SPAN: severity=error; text=Too bad there was little to no meat on the bone that followed...]\n[BR]\n[BR] Page 4 reveals that the villain's \"master plan\" amounts to little more than convincing unsuspecting Americans \"that their friends are really enemies... and their enemies... such as <i>I</i>... really their <i>friends</i>!\"&nbsp; He's even silly enough to believe that he could become like another Fidel Castro!\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=And plotter Stan Lee conjures up a \"mesmerizing wand\" to explain how such an unlikely thing seems plausible.]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(It doesn't work.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nIs it not bad enough that the Rabble Rouser is nothing more than an \"undercover Red agent?\"&nbsp; Must he also come equipped with a \"mesmerizing wand\" as well?&nbsp; Even worse is the fact that he uses a smaller version of the \"Red prototype sub-surface vehicle\" that Stan and Jack first debuted only three months ago in [LINK:1963:FF_21|FF #21|2027-01-23], featuring the Hate Monger (and Colonel Nick Fury).\n\nIn fact, this entire story feels like nothing more than a \"smaller version\" of that same plot.&nbsp; There is no \"hate ray\" here.&nbsp; <i>Nope!</i>  Only a \"mesmerizing wand.\"&nbsp; (The <i>delivery mechanism</i> may have changed, but the effect on the crowd was essentially the same -- it is stirred into a frenzy!)\n[ASSET: center | FF21retread | width=67%]\n\nIt's entirely irrelevant that \"Editor\" Stan Lee is big enough to <i>mention</i> the aforementioned FF issue in his editorial note.&nbsp; It's almost a repeat of the same plot -- [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=only in miniature].\n\nIt's almost not worth asking sensible questions such as:\n[HANG] * Where did this guy <i>get</i> the wand?\n[HANG] * How is he funded?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Remember, he has a \"lab.\")]\n[HANG] * How did he come into possession of a \"sub-surface vehicle\" like the one the Hate Monger used?\n[HANG] * How is this underground vehicle later able to hover in the air on page 8?\n[HANG] * [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=And why not throw this in too:]&nbsp; Where did this guy even <i>come </i>from?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(There is no backstory at <i>all</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n<i>Naturally</i>, the sub-surface Communist vehicle travels through \"old abandoned\" parts of the subway under the city streets after he abducts Prince Nagamo.&nbsp; (I'm beginning to think NYC is just littered with \"old abandoned\" things!)\n\n[ASSET: left | PermissionToFlameOn | width=14%] Only after the mayor granted Johnny permission to flame on does the story ever really advance past \"Communist-agent-tries-to-interfere-with-foreign-policy.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] But by this time, the story has advanced to page 9, while at the same time,\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=the reader has lost the will to live...]\n\nI'm telling you, by the time the Rabble Rouser was captured, I found myself no longer caring what happened.&nbsp; I was instead upset at how [SPAN: severity=error; text=dumb this story was... and sad that I was forced to read it.]\n[ASSET: center | LovingAmerica | width=67%]\n\n[HANG0] * The Rabble Rouser was unbelievably lame\n[HANG0] * The underground craft that bores through the ground below the city is still ill-conceived, so it's a shame Stan Lee used it a second time (though he claimed in that FF issue the technology was real).\n[HANG0] * The mesmerizer wand was just patently stupid and was also never explained.\n[HANG0] * A city ordinance against flaming on is wildly short-sighted (even if partially influenced by that aforementioned mesmerizer wand).\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=I could not turn the pages fast enough to get to the end of this tale and put it behind me!]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe only reason this one avoids a lower rating was the fact that a sympathetic Spider-Man shows up to try and lend aid.&nbsp; Johnny may have been all mouth when he said to get lost (only realizing afterward that Spider-Man really <i>was</i> trying to help), but I enjoyed seeing even this short glimpse of their friendly rivalry continued here.&nbsp; It was at least completely in character.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But at the same time, it reminds readers that there are <i>much</i> better comics out there!)]\n\nThe interesting staging in the opening pages was also new, though it was very <i>obviously</i> a writer shortcut.\n\nFrom an art perspective, Dick Ayers did an <i>adequate</i> job, though the brief appearance by Spider-Man on page 3 shows that he <b><i>really</i></b> went overboard on the webbing under his arms -- <i>wow</i>!\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Yeah, you guessed it:&nbsp; I'm trying to at least give credit where it's due... but it's <i>really</i> hard with this one...)]\n\nOther than those three things, I walk away from this one [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=very grateful] that it's behind me now...\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Ugh!]",
  "highlight": "The Human Torch battles the Rabble Rouser's campaign of mass persuasion, but readers may find themselves fighting to stay interested."
}